Z||000002||PaUL||07-08-99||06:17 PM||||$200 a pint? I guess only a true rice boy would spend that much just on paint.
Z||000003||four50four||07-08-99||06:18 PM||||seen the same on a 5.0. boy wuz it beautiful, like it made an ugly car look good, imagine what it could do to a nice looking car! REAllY expensive though!
Z||000004||lowCG||07-08-99||06:35 PM||||I was just talking to this guy on Sunday,who drives a DeTomaso Mangusta,paints and restores cars,said the Dupont version was a bit cheaper than the PPG,and it's real easy to over-do,and have a car that looks like a ghetto ride.
Z||000005||BobC||11-03-1999||11:01 PM||||The Imeron is what my Gold T/A Hood is done with.  Its some real pretty paint.  Not easy to spray i don't think, though.  Cost was $125 a qt. ||||reg||6
Z||000006||fiero56||11-03-1999||11:11 PM||||When I was at the national Corvette Museum several years ago, they had a 'vette that was red from the front, and had three or four different colors at various angles.  Very cool.  I prefer my stock black though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-03-1999||11:33 PM||||DuPont Imron is very hard to work with and dangerous. It is probably one of the most toxic paints there is. Imron is a 2 part "polyurethane enamel" paint. If not very carefully mixed and quickly used it can harden in the paint gun and pretty much ruin it.<P>You can use Imron to paint nearly anything. (One DuPont press release notes the Trump Taj Mahal in Atlantic City, NJ is painted with allot of it.) It is an incredibly tough paint. That I'm aware of there isn't a "soap bubble" version of it, but it will but a shine on a car topped only by very meticulus use of laqures or clearcoat finishes. Unlike either of those finishes only a very hard impact will damage it. (We used it to paint motorcycle frames. even after driving on gravel the frames looked new.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||jlhuber||11-03-1999||11:40 PM||||that color is actually quite easy to replicate, if you will settle for only two colors.  one of my friends paints cars as a hobby and says that the corvette was the frist to use this tinted clearcoat method on a white car with a bluish tint.  you have to use seperate paint and clearcoat, not that stinkin user friendly-acryllic-all-in-one shit.  lets say you want the popular green and purple.  to get the effect right you need to mix some purple in with the clearcoat, and of course the more you mix in the darker it will be.  you paint the car with the green, let it dry as you normally would, then sray on your clearcoat.  when all done, you will look at the car from one angle seeing the green paint, then step to the side at a diferent angle seeing the purple. (aka the tinted clearcoat)  i used green and purple as my example for it is the most common one i am seeing.  this is just another way to semi-reproduce a factory color changer.  there are a couple of drawbacks with this "poor-man's style" though, one- your ending clearcoat is a little bit duller due to the added color, and two- it only produces two colors.  the traditional green/rainbow effect is what ppg calls bavarian green, basf calls iridescent, dupont calls it imeron, ford used it on some cobras, and chevy used it on some montes.  i can't recall exactly what ford and chevy called it though.<P><p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 11-03-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000009||Cooter||11-04-1999||08:20 AM||||I thought the DuPont version of the color changing paint was Chroma-Illusion. The paint was introduced at the Winston a few years ago on Jeff Gordon's car. Really weird- changes from gold to reds, purples and blues. I saw a black 32 Ford coupe with ChromaIllusion flames on it. It looked really wild. Imron is a polyurethane enamal that, like Ogre said, is not something for the novice. Too much polyisocynates (cynide????).||||reg||5
Z||000010||fieroshop||11-04-1999||09:34 AM||||imron used to be the most toxic paint there was until they changed their formula. It is not the hardest paint to use and I personnally like using it due to the fact that it is extremely durable. We do a lot of painting at my shop, Imron is one of the paints we use along with PPG||||reg||1
Z||000011||Shiner||11-04-1999||12:33 PM||||I am considering Imron for when I repaint my GT.  My father, who is in the chemical buisness, said that they would take a piece of copper, a REAL thin piece, almost like paper, and paint it with Imron.  He said you could bend it back and forth and back and forth until the copper gave away and broke, but the paint would still be in one piece, and looking like new!!!  He also told me that it's a carcinogen, which means it's HIGHLY toxic and causes cancer.  I know alot of painters won't touch the stuff.||||reg||5
Z||000012||theogre||11-04-1999||01:44 PM||||I haven't used Imron in a number of years. If they've changed the formula that would help allot.<P>As for carcynogens.... you expose yourself to those everytime you work on the car. they are called gasoline and motor oil.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wolfhound||11-04-1999||03:03 PM||||Imron will hurt you. Use an good chemical mask at least or an air supply at best.<BR>ventilation is not enough, I painted a boat with Imron (outside)and basicly had a hangover for a week.||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||11-04-1999||03:46 PM||||We weren't saying it was safe, just safer maybe than it was thru the 80's. Back then even the best mask wasn't good enough. You had to use a forced air system.<P>There are no "safe" paints. even the water base stuff should be used with a mask.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Wolfhound||11-05-1999||06:07 AM||||Correct, Ogre, any improvement is good, but the skull and crossbones on the back of the Imron can doesn't mean it was made by pirates. The cost of equipment to make it safe is prohibitive for someone who is not a profesional painter.<BR>I will say it's some bullet proof paint. many beachings with the catamaran I painted failed to penetrate the paint.||||reg||7
